---
import BlogLayout from '../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import { posts, formatDate } from '../../data/blog.js';

const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === 'bezier-curves');
---

<BlogLayout
  title={`${post.title} · Rishikesh S`}
  description={post.summary}
>
  <main id="main">
    <article class="post">
      <header class="post-head">
        <div class="post-meta">
          <span class="ac">{formatDate(post.date)}</span>
          <span>{post.minutes} min read</span>
          <span>graphics · maths</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="post-title">Bézier curves from first principles</h1>
        <p class="post-standfirst">
          Every glyph in this sentence, every icon in your toolbar and the way
          this page's animations speed up and slow down all come from one small
          idea, applied twice. I finally sat down to understand it properly.
          These are my notes, built up from a straight line, with demos you can
          drag.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="post-body">
        <p>
          A bit of history first, because I enjoyed it. In 1962 Pierre Bézier
          was an engineer at Renault with a problem: car bodies were designed
          as physical clay models, and there was no good way to describe their
          surfaces to the milling machines that had to cut them. The curves he
          popularised were actually worked out a few years earlier by Paul de
          Casteljau at Citroën, who wasn't allowed to publish. The idea in both
          places was the same. Pin a curve down with a handful of points, and
          it can be reproduced exactly, anywhere, at any scale.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sixty years later the same family of curves is the backbone of
          computer graphics. Fonts, SVG icons, Illustrator's pen tool, CSS
          animation easing, robot motion planning. I knew the name for years
          without knowing how they actually work, and it turns out the
          construction is genuinely simple. The only prerequisite is knowing
          how to walk along a straight line.
        </p>

        <h2 id="lerp">Start with a line</h2>
        <p>
          Take two points, <strong>A</strong> and <strong>B</strong>, and a
          number <code>t</code> between 0 and 1. <em>Linear interpolation</em>
          (lerp, to its friends) gives you the point that is a fraction
          <code>t</code> of the way from A to B:
        </p>
        <pre class="math">lerp(A, B, t) = (1 − t) · A  +  t · B</pre>
        <p>
          At <code>t = 0</code> you're standing on A, at <code>t = 1</code>
          you're on B, at <code>t = 0.5</code> you're exactly halfway. Sweep
          <code>t</code> from 0 to 1 and you trace the whole segment. That's
          the entire toolbox. A straight line traced this way is technically
          already a Bézier curve, a degree-1 one with control points A and B.
          Everything that follows is just lerp applied to the results of other
          lerps, which honestly surprised me when it clicked.
        </p>

        <h2 id="de-casteljau">De Casteljau's algorithm</h2>
        <p>
          Now take <strong>three</strong> points: P₀, P₁ and P₂. For a given
          <code>t</code>, do this:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Lerp from P₀ to P₁, call the result Q₀.</li>
          <li>Lerp from P₁ to P₂ with the <em>same</em> t, call it Q₁.</li>
          <li>Lerp from Q₀ to Q₁. That point is on the curve.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Sweep <code>t</code> from 0 to 1 and that final point sweeps out a
          smooth arc: a <strong>quadratic Bézier curve</strong>. The recursive
          walk is called De Casteljau's algorithm. Try it below. Drag the
          points, scrub <code>t</code>, and watch the two-stage construction
          happen:
        </p>

        <figure class="demo" id="d1">
          <div class="demo-panel">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 640 360" aria-label="Interactive quadratic Bézier construction. Three draggable control points, with the De Casteljau construction lines drawn for the current value of t."></svg>
            <div class="demo-controls">
              <label>t
                <input type="range" class="demo-range" min="0" max="1" step="0.005" value="0.4" aria-label="Parameter t" />
              </label>
              <span class="readout" data-readout>t = 0.400</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <b>Fig. 1</b> · the two grey lerps produce the amber points; the
            amber lerp produces the point on the curve. Drag any control point;
            handles also move with arrow keys when focused.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The thing that made this click for me is what the amber line is
          doing: at every <code>t</code> it is <em>tangent</em> to the curve.
          The construction doesn't just give you points, it gives you
          directions for free. That's why the same machinery can drive font
          rendering and also steer a CNC cutter along a path.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cubic">One more level: the cubic</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing stops you at three points. With four control points you lerp
          three times to get three points, lerp those to get two, and lerp once
          more to land on the curve. That's a <strong>cubic Bézier</strong>,
          and it's the workhorse of the whole field. It's the cheapest curve
          that can hold an inflection (an S-bend), and you get independent
          control over the direction the curve leaves each endpoint.
        </p>

        <figure class="demo" id="d2">
          <div class="demo-panel">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 640 360" aria-label="Interactive cubic Bézier curve with four draggable control points and optional De Casteljau construction lines."></svg>
            <div class="demo-controls">
              <label>t
                <input type="range" class="demo-range" min="0" max="1" step="0.005" value="0.35" aria-label="Parameter t" />
              </label>
              <span class="readout" data-readout>t = 0.350</span>
              <span class="spacer"></span>
              <button class="demo-btn" data-cons aria-pressed="true">construction</button>
              <button class="demo-btn" data-play>play</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <b>Fig. 2</b> · a cubic is three nested layers of lerp. Try
            dragging P₁ across the line from P₀ to P₃ to put an S-bend in it.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Unrolling the recursion gives the closed form. For the cubic, collect
          the terms and each control point ends up weighted by a polynomial in
          <code>t</code>:
        </p>
        <pre class="math">B(t) = (1−t)³ P₀  +  3(1−t)²t P₁  +  3(1−t)t² P₂  +  t³ P₃</pre>
        <p>
          Those weights, <code>(1−t)³, 3(1−t)²t, 3(1−t)t², t³</code>, are the
          <strong>Bernstein basis polynomials</strong>, and two things about
          them are worth staring at. They are the terms of the binomial
          expansion of <code>((1−t) + t)³</code>, so at every <code>t</code>
          they sum to exactly 1: the curve point is always a weighted average
          of the control points. And each weight peaks at a different
          <code>t</code>, which is the precise sense in which P₁ "pulls" the
          early part of the curve and P₂ the late part. The curve passes
          through its endpoints (<code>B(0) = P₀</code>,
          <code>B(1) = P₃</code>) but only <em>approaches</em> the middle
          control points. It never touches them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The derivative falls straight out of the algebra, and it's another
          Bézier curve, one degree lower:
        </p>
        <pre class="math">B′(t) = 3(1−t)² (P₁−P₀)  +  6(1−t)t (P₂−P₁)  +  3t² (P₃−P₂)</pre>
        <p>
          At <code>t = 0</code> that collapses to <code>3(P₁−P₀)</code>: the
          curve leaves P₀ aimed directly at P₁. So a pen tool's "handles" are a
          literal picture of the derivative at each anchor. I'd been dragging
          them for years without knowing that.
        </p>

        <h2 id="properties">Why these curves won</h2>
        <p>
          Plenty of curve families existed before Bézier's. As far as I can
          tell, this one took over because a short list of properties happens
          to be exactly what designers, font engineers and renderers need:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The control points mean something.</strong> Endpoints are on
            the curve; handles set the tangents. You can hand the four points to
            a person with a mouse and they can steer the curve by feel. Try
            explaining a polynomial's coefficients to anyone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Convex hull.</strong> Because the Bernstein weights are
            non-negative and sum to 1, the curve can never escape the convex
            hull of its control points. A renderer can trivially bound a curve,
            cull it, or test it for intersection without evaluating anything.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Affine invariance.</strong> Rotate, scale or skew the
            control points and the curve transforms with them, exactly. You
            transform 4 points instead of 200 sampled ones, which is why a font
            scales from 8px to a billboard with no loss.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Variation diminishing.</strong> The curve wiggles no more
            than its control polygon does: a line crosses the curve at most as
            many times as it crosses the polygon. No surprise oscillations,
            which is what makes the curves feel predictable under the cursor.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cheap to evaluate, cheap to split.</strong> A handful of
            lerps per point, in fixed or floating point, no trigonometry
            anywhere.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="uses">Where you've already used them today</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Fonts.</strong> A glyph outline is a loop of Bézier segments.
          TrueType (<code>.ttf</code>) uses quadratics, picked in the late '80s
          because they're cheaper to rasterise, while PostScript and
          CFF/OpenType outlines use cubics. When this page loaded, your browser
          took the curve descriptions out of three font files and flattened
          several thousand of them into the pixels you're reading right now.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Vector graphics.</strong> SVG's path syntax exposes the
          machinery directly: <code>Q</code> is a quadratic segment,
          <code>C</code> a cubic. These two paths are figures 1 and 2 of this
          page, control point for control point:
        </p>
        <pre><code><span class="c">&lt;!-- one quadratic, one cubic: figures 1 and 2 --&gt;</span>
&lt;path d="<span class="k">M</span> 70 300  <span class="k">Q</span> 320 60   570 300" /&gt;
&lt;path d="<span class="k">M</span> 70 300  <span class="k">C</span> 180 70   460 70   570 300" /&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>
          Every pen tool, whether it's in Illustrator, Figma, Inkscape or
          Glyphs, is an editor for exactly these commands. When you drag a
          handle and the curve follows, you are moving P₁ in figure 2.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Animation easing.</strong> CSS's
          <code>cubic-bezier(x1, y1, x2, y2)</code> is a cubic in disguise, with
          a twist: the axes aren't space, they're <em>time</em> (x) against
          <em>progress</em> (y). P₀ is pinned at (0,0) and P₃ at (1,1); you only
          get to place the middle two points. The browser then answers "at 40%
          of the duration, how far along is the animation?" by intersecting the
          curve with a vertical line. That's why the spec clamps x₁ and x₂ to
          [0,1]: time isn't allowed to flow backwards. The y values are
          unclamped, and pushing them outside [0,1] is how you get overshoot and
          bounce.
        </p>

        <figure class="demo" id="d3">
          <div class="demo-panel">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 640 400" aria-label="Interactive CSS easing curve editor. Two draggable control points shape a cubic Bézier timing function plotted as progress against time."></svg>
            <div class="ease-preview" aria-hidden="true">
              <div class="track"></div>
              <div class="ball"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="demo-controls">
              <span class="readout" data-readout style="min-width:23ch">cubic-bezier(.22, .70, .20, 1)</span>
              <span class="spacer"></span>
              <button class="demo-btn" data-preset="0,0,1,1">linear</button>
              <button class="demo-btn" data-preset=".25,.1,.25,1">ease</button>
              <button class="demo-btn" data-preset=".42,0,.58,1">in-out</button>
              <button class="demo-btn" data-preset=".22,.7,.2,1">this site</button>
              <button class="demo-btn" data-run>run ▸</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <b>Fig. 3</b> · drag the two handles, then <b>run</b> to feel the
            easing. The default is this site's own transition curve,
            <b>--ease</b> in its stylesheet. Drag a handle's y outside the plot
            to make the dot overshoot.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <strong>Everything that moves along a path.</strong> Camera rails in
          games, GPS route smoothing, the toolpath of a 3D printer, PowerPoint's
          motion paths, robot arm trajectories. Any time something needs to get
          from A to B without a visible kink, there's a good chance a cubic
          Bézier is doing the steering.
        </p>

        <h2 id="rendering">How a curve becomes pixels</h2>
        <p>
          A GPU rasteriser wants straight lines, so at some point every Bézier
          on your screen gets <em>flattened</em> into a polyline. The naive way
          is to sample a fixed number of <code>t</code> values, but that wastes
          segments on the straight-ish parts and starves the tight bends. The
          standard answer uses a property of De Casteljau's construction that I
          did not see coming: the intermediate points from figure 2 don't just
          locate one point, <strong>they split the curve into two smaller
          Béziers</strong> whose control points hug the curve more tightly than
          the original four.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the renderer recurses: if a curve's control points are within
          tolerance of a straight line, emit a segment; otherwise split at
          <code>t = ½</code> and repeat. Flat regions terminate early, bends
          subdivide deeper, and the error is bounded everywhere:
        </p>

        <figure class="demo" id="d4">
          <div class="demo-panel">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 640 360" aria-label="Interactive demonstration of adaptive flattening. A cubic Bézier is recursively subdivided into straight segments; a tolerance slider controls how many."></svg>
            <div class="demo-controls">
              <label>tolerance
                <input type="range" class="demo-range" min="0.15" max="24" step="0.05" value="6" aria-label="Flatness tolerance in pixels" />
              </label>
              <span class="readout" data-readout style="min-width:12ch">12 segments</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <b>Fig. 4</b> · adaptive subdivision. Tighten the tolerance and
            segments appear exactly where the curvature is; drag the points and
            watch the subdivision redistribute itself.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 id="limits">What they can't do</h2>
        <p>
          Two honest limitations, and what the field does about them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>No polynomial curve is a circle.</strong> A cubic can only
          approximate an arc. The classic trick pins a cubic to a quarter
          circle and asks how long the handles should be; the answer is
          <code>k ≈ 0.5523 · r</code>, a number quietly hard-coded into nearly
          every graphics library that offers rounded corners. The error over a
          quarter circle is about 0.02% of the radius. Invisible at any screen
          size, which is why nobody minds.
        </p>

        <figure class="demo">
          <div class="demo-panel">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 640 420" aria-label="A quarter circle drawn as a single cubic Bézier. The two handles have length 0.5523 times the radius." id="circle-fig"></svg>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <b>Fig. 5</b> · one cubic (solid) laid over a true circle (faint).
            Each handle is tangent to the circle with length k·r, k ≈ 0.5523.
            Four of these make the "circles" in most icon sets.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <strong>One curve doesn't scale to complex shapes.</strong> You could
          raise the degree to ten or eleven control points, but high-degree
          Béziers are miserable: moving any single point bends the
          <em>entire</em> curve, and the shape drifts ever further from its
          control polygon. So practice goes the other way: keep the pieces cubic
          and <strong>chain them</strong>. As long as each joint's incoming and
          outgoing handles are collinear, the seam is invisible. That is
          exactly what a pen tool's "smooth point" enforces, and why breaking
          the handles gives you a corner. A chain of Béziers is a
          <em>spline</em>. Add per-point weights so arcs come out exact and you
          get NURBS, the standard in CAD, and a direct descendant of what
          Bézier and de Casteljau built for car bodies.
        </p>

        <h2 id="further">Further reading</h2>
        <p>Things I leaned on while figuring this out:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="https://pomax.github.io/bezierinfo/" rel="noopener">A Primer on Bézier Curves</a>,
            Pomax's free book. The reference for everything this post skipped
            (arc length, offsetting, intersections).
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aVwxzDHniEw" rel="noopener">The Beauty of Bézier Curves</a>,
            Freya Holmér's 24-minute video. The best visual treatment of
            splines and continuity I've found anywhere.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://raphlinus.github.io/curves/2018/12/28/bezier-arclength.html" rel="noopener">Raph Levien's curve notes</a>,
            for when the practical questions (arc length, flattening error
            bounds) get serious.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          <a href="/">← All writing</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <script>
    /* All four figures share one tiny toolkit: lerp, De Casteljau, an SVG
       builder, and one drag/keyboard controller for handles. No libraries —
       the page stays as dependency-free as the site it lives on. */

    const SVGNS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
    const lerp = (a, b, t) => [a[0] + (b[0] - a[0]) * t, a[1] + (b[1] - a[1]) * t];

    /* one De Casteljau step: n points in, n−1 out */
    const step = (pts, t) => pts.slice(1).map((p, i) => lerp(pts[i], p, t));

    function bezierPoint(pts, t) {
      let level = pts;
      while (level.length > 1) level = step(level, t);
      return level[0];
    }

    function curvePath(pts, from = 0, to = 1, n = 48) {
      let d = '';
      for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        const p = bezierPoint(pts, from + ((to - from) * i) / n);
        d += (i ? 'L' : 'M') + p[0].toFixed(2) + ' ' + p[1].toFixed(2);
      }
      return d;
    }

    function el(svg, tag, attrs) {
      const node = document.createElementNS(SVGNS, tag);
      for (const k in attrs) node.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]);
      svg.appendChild(node);
      return node;
    }

    function toSvg(svg, clientX, clientY) {
      const pt = new DOMPoint(clientX, clientY);
      return pt.matrixTransform(svg.getScreenCTM().inverse());
    }

    /* A handle: transparent hit area + visible dot, movable by pointer or by
       arrow keys when focused. `clamp` keeps it inside its figure's rules. */
    function makeHandle(svg, label, get, set, clamp, onChange) {
      const g = document.createElementNS(SVGNS, 'g');
      g.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
      /* not role="slider": a 2D handle has no single value axis, so it is a
         button whose label carries its position */
      g.setAttribute('role', 'button');
      /* 26 svg units ≈ a 40px touch target once the 640-unit viewBox is
         squeezed to a phone column */
      const hit = el(g, 'circle', { class: 'handle-hit', r: 26 });
      el(g, 'circle', { class: 'handle', r: 6 });
      svg.appendChild(g);

      function position() {
        const p = get();
        g.setAttribute('transform', `translate(${p[0]} ${p[1]})`);
        g.setAttribute('aria-label', `${label} — drag or use arrow keys. At x ${Math.round(p[0])}, y ${Math.round(p[1])}`);
      }

      function move(x, y) {
        set(clamp([x, y]));
        position();
        onChange();
      }

      g.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => {
        e.preventDefault();
        g.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
        const onMove = (ev) => {
          const p = toSvg(svg, ev.clientX, ev.clientY);
          move(p.x, p.y);
        };
        const onUp = (ev) => {
          g.releasePointerCapture(ev.pointerId);
          g.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);
          g.removeEventListener('pointerup', onUp);
        };
        g.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove);
        g.addEventListener('pointerup', onUp);
      });

      g.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
        const d = e.shiftKey ? 1 : 8;
        const p = get();
        const delta = { ArrowLeft: [-d, 0], ArrowRight: [d, 0], ArrowUp: [0, -d], ArrowDown: [0, d] }[e.key];
        if (!delta) return;
        e.preventDefault();
        move(p[0] + delta[0], p[1] + delta[1]);
      });

      position();
      return { position, hit };
    }

    const inBox = ([x, y]) => [Math.min(616, Math.max(24, x)), Math.min(336, Math.max(24, y))];
    const fmt = (t) => 't = ' + t.toFixed(3);
    const polyPoints = (pts) => pts.map((p) => p.join(',')).join(' ');
    const labelFor = ['P₀', 'P₁', 'P₂', 'P₃'];

    /* ---------- figures 1 & 2: the construction ---------- */
    function constructionFigure(id, pts, showConsDefault) {
      const fig = document.getElementById(id);
      const svg = fig.querySelector('svg');
      const range = fig.querySelector('input[type="range"]');
      const readout = fig.querySelector('[data-readout]');
      const consBtn = fig.querySelector('[data-cons]');
      const playBtn = fig.querySelector('[data-play]');
      let t = parseFloat(range.value);
      let showCons = showConsDefault;

      const poly = el(svg, 'polyline', { class: 'poly' });
      const faint = el(svg, 'path', { class: 'curve-faint' });
      const traced = el(svg, 'path', { class: 'curve' });
      /* enough construction elements for a cubic: two levels of lines/dots */
      const consLines = [el(svg, 'polyline', { class: 'cons' }), el(svg, 'polyline', { class: 'cons', opacity: 0.55 })];
      const consDots = [];
      for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) consDots.push(el(svg, 'circle', { class: 'cons-dot', r: 3.5 }));
      const onCurve = el(svg, 'circle', { class: 'on-curve', r: 5.5 });
      const labels = pts.map((p, i) =>
        el(svg, 'text', { class: 'glabel', x: 0, y: 0, 'text-anchor': 'middle' })
      );
      labels.forEach((node, i) => (node.textContent = labelFor[i]));

      function render() {
        poly.setAttribute('points', polyPoints(pts));
        faint.setAttribute('d', curvePath(pts));
        traced.setAttribute('d', curvePath(pts, 0, t));

        let dot = 0;
        let level = pts;
        for (let depth = 0; level.length > 2; depth++) {
          level = step(level, t);
          consLines[depth].setAttribute('points', polyPoints(level));
          consLines[depth].style.display = showCons ? '' : 'none';
          for (const p of level) {
            consDots[dot].setAttribute('cx', p[0]);
            consDots[dot].setAttribute('cy', p[1]);
            consDots[dot].style.display = showCons ? '' : 'none';
            dot++;
          }
        }
        for (; dot < consDots.length; dot++) consDots[dot].style.display = 'none';
        if (pts.length === 3) consLines[1].style.display = 'none';

        const b = bezierPoint(pts, t);
        onCurve.setAttribute('cx', b[0]);
        onCurve.setAttribute('cy', b[1]);

        labels.forEach((node, i) => {
          const p = pts[i];
          node.setAttribute('x', p[0]);
          /* keep the label on the far side of the curve: above for the top
             points, below for the bottom ones */
          node.setAttribute('y', p[1] < 180 ? p[1] - 16 : p[1] + 26);
        });
        readout.textContent = fmt(t);
      }

      const handles = pts.map((_, i) =>
        makeHandle(svg, `Control point ${labelFor[i]}`, () => pts[i], (v) => (pts[i] = v), inBox, render)
      );

      range.addEventListener('input', () => {
        t = parseFloat(range.value);
        stopPlay();
        render();
      });

      /* play sweeps t at a fixed rate; any interaction hands control back */
      let raf = 0;
      function stopPlay() {
        if (!raf) return;
        cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
        raf = 0;
        if (playBtn) playBtn.textContent = 'play';
      }
      if (playBtn) {
        playBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (raf) return stopPlay();
          playBtn.textContent = 'stop';
          let last = performance.now();
          let dir = t >= 1 ? -1 : 1;
          const tick = (now) => {
            const dt = (now - last) / 1000;
            last = now;
            t += dir * dt * 0.45;
            if (t >= 1) { t = 1; dir = -1; }
            if (t <= 0) { t = 0; dir = 1; }
            range.value = String(t);
            render();
            raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
          };
          raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
        });
      }
      if (consBtn) {
        consBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          showCons = !showCons;
          consBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(showCons));
          render();
        });
      }
      render();
      return { render };
    }

    constructionFigure('d1', [[70, 300], [320, 60], [570, 300]], true);
    constructionFigure('d2', [[70, 300], [180, 70], [460, 70], [570, 300]], true);

    /* ---------- figure 3: the easing editor ---------- */
    (function easingFigure() {
      const fig = document.getElementById('d3');
      const svg = fig.querySelector('svg');
      const readout = fig.querySelector('[data-readout]');
      const ball = fig.querySelector('.ball');
      const preview = fig.querySelector('.ease-preview');

      /* unit square → svg. y is flipped, and the plot leaves headroom so
         overshoot (y outside [0,1]) stays visible. */
      const X0 = 90, X1 = 550, Y0 = 320, Y1 = 90;
      const toPx = ([u, v]) => [X0 + u * (X1 - X0), Y0 + v * (Y1 - Y0)];
      const toUnit = ([x, y]) => [(x - X0) / (X1 - X0), (y - Y0) / (Y1 - Y0)];

      let c = [0.22, 0.7, 0.2, 1]; /* the site's own --ease */

      el(svg, 'line', { class: 'axis', x1: X0, y1: Y0, x2: X1, y2: Y0 });
      el(svg, 'line', { class: 'axis', x1: X0, y1: Y0, x2: X0, y2: Y1 });
      el(svg, 'line', { class: 'axis', x1: X0, y1: Y1, x2: X1, y2: Y1, 'stroke-dasharray': '3 5' });
      el(svg, 'line', { class: 'axis', x1: X1, y1: Y0, x2: X1, y2: Y1, 'stroke-dasharray': '3 5' });
      const xl = el(svg, 'text', { class: 'glabel', x: X1 + 8, y: Y0 + 4 });
      xl.textContent = 'time →';
      const yl = el(svg, 'text', { class: 'glabel', x: X0 - 8, y: Y1 - 14, 'text-anchor': 'end' });
      yl.textContent = 'progress ↑';

      const arm1 = el(svg, 'polyline', { class: 'poly' });
      const arm2 = el(svg, 'polyline', { class: 'poly' });
      const curve = el(svg, 'path', { class: 'curve' });
      el(svg, 'circle', { class: 'on-curve', r: 4, cx: toPx([0, 0])[0], cy: toPx([0, 0])[1] });
      el(svg, 'circle', { class: 'on-curve', r: 4, cx: toPx([1, 1])[0], cy: toPx([1, 1])[1] });

      function cssValue() {
        const trim = (n) => {
          const s = (Math.round(n * 100) / 100).toString();
          return s.replace(/^(-?)0\./, '$1.');
        };
        return `cubic-bezier(${trim(c[0])}, ${trim(c[1])}, ${trim(c[2])}, ${trim(c[3])})`;
      }

      function render() {
        const P = [toPx([0, 0]), toPx([c[0], c[1]]), toPx([c[2], c[3]]), toPx([1, 1])];
        curve.setAttribute('d', curvePath(P));
        arm1.setAttribute('points', polyPoints([P[0], P[1]]));
        arm2.setAttribute('points', polyPoints([P[3], P[2]]));
        readout.textContent = cssValue();
        h1.position();
        h2.position();
      }

      /* time may not run backwards: x clamps to [0,1]; y may overshoot */
      const clampUnit = (p) => {
        const [u, v] = toUnit(p);
        return toPx([Math.min(1, Math.max(0, u)), Math.min(1.28, Math.max(-0.28, v))]);
      };
      const h1 = makeHandle(svg, 'Easing control point 1', () => toPx([c[0], c[1]]),
        (p) => { const [u, v] = toUnit(p); c[0] = u; c[1] = v; }, clampUnit, render);
      const h2 = makeHandle(svg, 'Easing control point 2', () => toPx([c[2], c[3]]),
        (p) => { const [u, v] = toUnit(p); c[2] = u; c[3] = v; }, clampUnit, render);

      fig.querySelectorAll('[data-preset]').forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          c = btn.dataset.preset.split(',').map(Number);
          render();
        });
      });

      /* run: let the browser itself do the easing, so what you feel is the
         real thing, not a re-implementation of it */
      let out = false;
      fig.querySelector('[data-run]').addEventListener('click', () => {
        const dist = preview.clientWidth - 32;
        ball.style.transition = 'none';
        ball.style.transform = out ? `translateX(${dist}px)` : 'translateX(0)';
        void ball.offsetWidth; /* commit the start position before easing */
        ball.style.transition = `transform 900ms ${cssValue()}`;
        ball.style.transform = out ? 'translateX(0)' : `translateX(${dist}px)`;
        out = !out;
      });

      render();
    })();

    /* ---------- figure 4: adaptive flattening ---------- */
    (function flattenFigure() {
      const fig = document.getElementById('d4');
      const svg = fig.querySelector('svg');
      const range = fig.querySelector('input[type="range"]');
      const readout = fig.querySelector('[data-readout]');
      const pts = [[70, 300], [150, 50], [420, 330], [570, 90]];

      const poly = el(svg, 'polyline', { class: 'poly' });
      const faint = el(svg, 'path', { class: 'curve-faint' });
      const segs = el(svg, 'path', { class: 'flat-seg' });
      const dots = el(svg, 'g', {});

      /* split via De Casteljau at t = ½: the intermediate points ARE the
         control points of the two halves */
      function split(p) {
        const q = step(p, 0.5), r = step(q, 0.5), s = step(r, 0.5);
        return [[p[0], q[0], r[0], s[0]], [s[0], r[1], q[2], p[3]]];
      }
      function flatEnough(p, tol) {
        const [a, , , b] = [p[0], p[1], p[2], p[3]];
        const [dx, dy] = [b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1]];
        const len = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;
        const dist = (q) => Math.abs((q[0] - a[0]) * dy - (q[1] - a[1]) * dx) / len;
        return Math.max(dist(p[1]), dist(p[2])) <= tol;
      }
      function flatten(p, tol, out, depth) {
        if (depth > 10 || flatEnough(p, tol)) { out.push(p[3]); return; }
        const [l, r] = split(p);
        flatten(l, tol, out, depth + 1);
        flatten(r, tol, out, depth + 1);
      }

      function render() {
        const tol = parseFloat(range.value);
        poly.setAttribute('points', polyPoints(pts));
        faint.setAttribute('d', curvePath(pts, 0, 1, 96));
        const verts = [pts[0]];
        flatten(pts, tol, verts, 0);
        segs.setAttribute('d', verts.map((p, i) => (i ? 'L' : 'M') + p[0].toFixed(1) + ' ' + p[1].toFixed(1)).join(''));
        dots.textContent = '';
        for (const p of verts) el(dots, 'circle', { class: 'flat-dot', r: 3, cx: p[0], cy: p[1] });
        readout.textContent = (verts.length - 1) + ' segments';
      }

      pts.forEach((_, i) =>
        makeHandle(svg, `Control point ${labelFor[i]}`, () => pts[i], (v) => (pts[i] = v), inBox, render)
      );
      range.addEventListener('input', render);
      render();
    })();

    /* ---------- figure 5: the circle constant (static) ---------- */
    (function circleFigure() {
      const svg = document.getElementById('circle-fig');
      const cx = 320, cy = 225, r = 165, k = 0.5523;
      /* true circle, faint */
      el(svg, 'circle', { class: 'curve-faint', cx, cy, r, stroke: 'var(--line-2)' });
      /* quarter arc as one cubic: (cx+r, cy) → (cx, cy−r) */
      const P = [[cx + r, cy], [cx + r, cy - k * r], [cx + k * r, cy - r], [cx, cy - r]];
      el(svg, 'path', { class: 'curve', d: curvePath(P, 0, 1, 64) });
      el(svg, 'polyline', { class: 'cons', points: polyPoints([P[0], P[1]]) });
      el(svg, 'polyline', { class: 'cons', points: polyPoints([P[3], P[2]]) });
      for (const p of [P[1], P[2]]) el(svg, 'circle', { class: 'cons-dot', r: 3.5, cx: p[0], cy: p[1] });
      for (const p of [P[0], P[3]]) el(svg, 'circle', { class: 'on-curve', r: 4.5, cx: p[0], cy: p[1] });
      const t1 = el(svg, 'text', { class: 'glabel', x: cx + r + 14, y: cy - (k * r) / 2, 'text-anchor': 'start' });
      t1.textContent = 'k·r';
      const t2 = el(svg, 'text', { class: 'glabel', x: cx + (k * r) / 2, y: cy - r - 12, 'text-anchor': 'middle' });
      t2.textContent = 'k·r';
    })();
  </script>
</BlogLayout>
